<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Nassau-Saarbrücken</h1><p class="st-meta">1381 CE – 1797 CE · capital Saarbrücken</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Nassau county on the Saar whose 18th-century princes made Saarbrücken a Baroque residence and an early centre of coal mining.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a> passed by marriage to the Walramian Nassaus when Count John I of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau-weilburg">County of Nassau-Weilburg</a> married its heiress <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-anjouan">Johanna</a>. In 1381 their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-count-of-nassau-saarbrucken">Philip I</a> inherited it, and Saarbrücken became the seat of a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau">Nassau</a> line that ruled lands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saar-river">Saar</a> and in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Lorraine</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Elisabeth of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Lorraine</a>, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-count-of-nassau-saarbrucken">Philip I</a>, translated French chansons de geste into German prose in the 15th century. The counts introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Lutheran Reformation</a> in 1575. In the 18th century Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-prince-of-nassau-saarbrucken">William Henry</a> and his son Louis made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> residence, with buildings by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-joachim-stengel">Friedrich Joachim Stengel</a> including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwigskirche">Ludwigskirche</a>, and developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining and ironworks.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French revolutionary troops occupied the county in 1793, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 1797. In 1815 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>; it is now the capital of the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarland">Saarland</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-nassau-saarbrucken.jpg" alt="County of Nassau-Saarbrücken" width="320" height="237"><figcaption>Image: Gustav Droysen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zweibrucken-Bitsch_around_1400_(134037786).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1381 CE – 1797 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saarbrücken</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philip I, William Henry, Louis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nassau-Saarbrücken, County of Saarbrücken</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1589&amp;focus=county-of-nassau-saarbrucken" aria-label="County of Nassau-Saarbrücken on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4841 461 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Nassau-Saarbrücken lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5161 717L5229 694M5161 717L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5161 717h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5161 717h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Saarbrücken. <a href="/atlas?year=1589&amp;focus=county-of-nassau-saarbrucken">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

